addclaim(101,923,7,[102],'309–315','Paraphrase: if transformations between exact functors are determined on S, does S generate every object by the chosen operations?','No for the empty doctrine, and no for finite products. Determination on a retract-dense basis is weaker than generation by the prescribed operations. This counterexample does not refute a separately strengthened finite-limit version.','open on the pages','For no operations, take the Karoubi completion of the one-object monoid {1,e}, e²=e≠1. Its new retract object is not isomorphic to the original object, but a transformation’s component on it is determined by the component on the original object using the retraction. For finite products take R=FinSet^op and S={2}. Since1 is a retract of2, its component is determined; every n is a finite power of1 in R. Yet products of2 in R have even cardinality2m (including0), so1 and3 are outside the product closure.','Kelly5.36 identifies retract completion; Adámek–Lawvere–Rosický2003p36/Proposition2.6 gives the same even-finite-set Cauchy phenomenon. The exact transformation-detection counterexamples answer the sheet’s question independently.',['Kelly1982','AdamekLawvereRosicky2003'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-101')
addclaim(102,923,7,[103],'317–358','Paraphrase: changing the doctrine gives a free envelope, perhaps an equivalence on Hom categories.','Restriction along a doctrine inclusion is represented by the corresponding free completion when it exists. The finite-limit to all-limit passage uses Pro completion, and the dual finite-colimit passage uses Ind. Restriction can be fully faithful on transformations between given finite constructions without making every construction on complete targets finite.','free-completion claims sketched; Hom-equivalence explicitly questioned','A construction X↦X^N commutes with every continuous functor between complete categories. It is not a finite-limit construction of a bare object: on Set a finite such construction is a finite power, and2^N is infinite while every2^n is finite. The free-completion universal property identifies modifications between the existing finite constructions; it does not make this new object of the Hom category lie in the image. All Pro/Ind uses retain the smallness or universe enlargement needed for the completion.','Kelly5.35 and5.10 give the completion/Ind machinery; the infinite-power witness is independently checked. This matches the manuscript’s earlier definition of 1-faithful at file05:328–330, and avoids conflating full faithfulness with essential surjectivity.',['Kelly1982'])
claims[-1]['lines'].append(source_file(5)+':326–330');claims[-1]['scans'] += [79]
addclaim(103,923,7,[103,104],'359–393','Paraphrase: opposite categories exchange limit and colimit doctrines and dualize types.','For a doctrine lambda=(lambda_limits,lambda_colimits), opposite categories exchange the two families, and the type-level dual is T^op(C)=T(C^op)^op, with the appropriate reversal of natural-transformation directions.','construction stated','Opposite reverses every cone/cocone and sends an exact functor to an exact functor for the dual doctrine. Applying opposite twice is canonically the identity. A natural transformation F⇒G becomes G^op⇒F^op, so the two-category convention must include reversal of2-cells; it is not a literal strict covariant endofunctor of unmodified Cat.','Kelly’s enriched opposite and universal-property framework and Di Liberti–Osmond’s explicit pseudofunctor convention provide the checked comparison; the elementary duality is independently verified.',['Kelly1982','DiLibertiOsmond2025'])
addclaim(104,924,7,[107],'555–595','Paraphrase: recover a distinguished category Sigma of constructions intrinsically from S=T(Set).','The evaluation copy R⊂Fun(S,Set) is recoverable when the chosen theory and its representable constructions are retained. The proposed intrinsic Sigma test on these lines still quantifies over T and arbitrary target categories and therefore is not a definition from the bare category S alone.','unfinished argument; intrinsic characterization not proved','C077 proves the full-faithful evaluation embedding. Closure under the dual operation shapes follows because representing presheaves are closed under the original limits. But the later criterion uses T(C), exact functors and their evaluations, so it has not eliminated T. For finite limits, C083 recovers R from S_fp; for finite products, C085 shows the remaining Morita/presentation choice.','Gabriel–Ulmer in Lack–Power8 and the ALR2003 Cauchy/Morita theorem settle those two precise special cases. Searches of those reconstruction sources and Kelly’s density theory did not identify the unfinished arbitrary-lambda criterion as a complete intrinsic theorem.',['LackPower2009','AdamekLawvereRosicky2003','Kelly1982'],'C')
addclaim(105,925,7,[109],'597–644','Paraphrase: a Hom-to-natural-transformations comparison is initially called a bijection, then an application, and proved bijective for representables.','For B=PSh(C), C small finitely complete, and f:E→B, the displayed comparison Hom_E(U,f*X)→Nat(O_B(X),O_E(U)) is natural in X,U. It is bijective for X representable, but not for arbitrary X.','corrected by himself on the same sheet: bijection deleted at614; representable restriction at622','For X=y(c), O_B(y(c)) is the covariant representable Hom_C(c,-), so Yoneda gives Nat(O_B(y(c)),O_E(U))≃Hom_E(U,f0(c)). For C=1, E=B=Set, f=id, U=1 and X=empty, the left side has0 elements while both O-functors are the unique terminal lex functor and the right side has1.','The exact representable argument is ordinary Yoneda (Riehl§2.2); the0-versus1 counterexample checks the necessity of the manuscript’s own correction. No full embedding of all B into the model-functor category is inferred.',['RiehlContextOnline'])
addclaim(106,925,7,[110],'645–684','Paraphrase: extend a fibred category to Pro(C) and then pull it to E to obtain an inverse-image stack.','For a left-exact site functor f0:C→E and a contravariant stack F on C, the inverse image is obtained by stackifying the bicategorical Kan-extension presheaf U↦2-colim_(c,U→f0(c))^op F(c). Site continuity and the chosen topology remain part of the hypotheses.','construction sketched; variance switches to cofibred near the end','The category I_U of pairs(c,U→f0c) is cofiltered: terminal object, products and equalizers follow from finite-limit preservation by Hom_E(U,f0−). F is contravariant, so the actual filtered colimit is over I_U^op. Localization of the appropriate fibred category gives the prestack universal property; stackification imposes descent. This is a bicategorical colimit, not an ordinary colimit of object sets.','Stacks04WA, Lemmas8.12.5–8.12.10, constructs the localization, its universal property, and pullback by stackification. The comma formula is the present explicit Kan-extension reconstruction; it includes the source’s subsequent subsite/topology variant.',['StacksPullback','VistoliDescent'])
addclaim(107,926,7,[112],'687–712','Paraphrase: structures on fixed objects should be classified by B×_(B_I)X; existence is not yet proved.','Let B classify a geometric theory with chosen underlying objects b_i and let E_i∈X. If b:B→B_I and E:X→B_I classify these families, the relative classifier is the bicategorical pullback B×_(B_I)X. It classifies a structure together with specified isomorphisms of its underlying objects with the pulled-back E_i.','universal property identified; existence explicitly open','A geometric morphism Y→the pullback is a pair Y→B,Y→X with an invertible comparison of their composites to B_I. Since B_I classifies I-families of objects, that comparison is exactly the chosen underlying-object isomorphism. This proves the represented functor once the pullback exists; Grothendieck-topos2-pullbacks supply existence.','SGA4IV Exercise9.1.6(d), original printed435, states existence of topos base change. Vickers2017 Theorems5.11–5.12pp242–244 give relative classifying toposes and their geometric base-change property for theory extensions. The exact fixed-object pullback is independently derived from that universal property.',['SGA4I1972','Vickers2017'],'A')
addclaim(108,921,6,[90],'339','« Q. Relation avec analyseurs de Lazard. »','The requested comparison concerns Lazard’s graded polynomial-operation systems and their formal completions. It is not an identified equivalence with arbitrary finite-limit sketches; a specific translation of the operation, substitution and completion data is still required.','open comparison question','Lazard§§4–6 starts with multigraded polynomial operations on modules, substitutions and neutral-variable relations, then passes to completed products of homogeneous components. The manuscript’s general finite-limit categories do not automatically include this grading or completion. Ordinary finite-product algebraic operations supply a common part; the infinitary completion is extra structure.','Lazard’s Bourbaki109 was delivered February1955 and published in the1956 volume, pp77–91; §§4–6 were opened and read. The full1955AnnENS article is the backward bibliographic reference. Bénabou1968 cites both Lawvere and Lazard, but only its bibliography was inspected, so it is not used as a proved equivalence.',['Lazard1956'],'C',confidence='medium')
addclaim(109,910,3,[37],'48–53;90–92;123–133','Paraphrase: noetherian, regular, Cohen–Macaulay, Gorenstein, complete-intersection and finite-presentation conditions receive negative annotations.','As full ring properties with all ring maps, noetherianity, the usual noetherian regular/CM/Gorenstein/complete-intersection conditions, finite type/presentation over a fixed base, artinianness and finiteness of the idempotent set fail filtered-colimit closure. They are therefore not geometric full subtheories in that interpretation.','negative annotations, several with question marks; no proof','The sequence k[x1,…,xn] has finite-type regular complete-intersection stages, but colimit k[x1,x2,…] is not noetherian and not finitely generated. For artinianness and finitely many idempotents take finite Boolean rings F2^{2^n} with the duplication embeddings; their colimit is the Boolean ring of clopen cylinder sets on Cantor space, with infinitely many idempotents and descending nonstabilizing ideals. This does not address alternative nonnoetherian definitions of regularity or CM, which the fragment does not specify.','The geometric filtered-colimit criterion is the same one checked in C027. Stacks flatness and finite-presentation criteria distinguish these finiteness conditions from geometric flatness; the explicit directed-system witnesses supply the negative answers.',['SGA4I1972','StacksFlatness'],'C')
addclaim(110,908,2,[24],'168–174','Paraphrase: the Set-model category may be empty even for a nonempty classifying topos.','There are nondegenerate Grothendieck topoi with no Set-valued points. Thus absence of Set models does not mean that the geometric theory is inconsistent in all topoi.','stated without an example','Let B be a complete atomless Boolean algebra, for example regular opens of R. A frame map B→2 preserves complements and arbitrary joins, hence arbitrary meets. The meet of its1-fiber would be a nonzero atom, impossible. Therefore Sh(B) has no points, while0≠1 in its subterminal frame proves nondegeneracy. The associated propositional geometric theory has B-valued models despite no Set model.','Barr1974, Toposes without points, Introductionp265 gives this exact complete-Boolean-algebra example and argument, with a backward reference to SGA4IV7.4. Its stronger Boolean-localization theorem is not needed here.',['Barr1974'])
addclaim(111,927,2,[34],'540–544','Paraphrase of detached formulas only: H^n(K(G,1), uncertain coefficient), an arrow to simplicial sets, and elsewhere nested P1,P2 with a question mark.','No single precise proposition is identifiable from these detached expressions. The cohomology coefficient, degree-map type and direction are unclear; the ring-topology fragments do not specify P1,P2 or the displayed n-model limit.','fragmentary formulas, question mark and canceled symbols','The surrounding text establishes neither the missing definitions nor a typed assertion. Classical group cohomology of K(G,1), simplicial Eilenberg–Mac Lane constructions, and the adjacent ring-topology discussion were searched as leads; none licenses choosing one reading. The expressions are retained verbatim in the archival source and registered for ink/context checking.','Searches of group-cohomology/K(G,1) and the adjacent published classifying-topos/ring-site literature found several possible frameworks but no exact match to this incomplete fragment. This is classF identification failure, not a claim of a new open theorem.',['SGA4I1972','Lazard1956'],'F',confidence='low',event='carried as background without fault found')
